# suopTranscriber local patch — sherpa-onnx speaker diarization pipeline:
# batching, progress reporting, and follow-up fixes for real-world
# multi-hour recordings.
#
# 1. Batch inference calls (segmentation + embedding models) instead of
#    running one tiny Ort::Session::Run() per sliding window / per speech
#    segment — measured ~10% GPU utilization on real recordings even after
#    enabling CUDA. Segmentation batching is straightforward (fixed window
#    size). Embedding batching pads variable-length segments, bucketing
#    same-length-ish segments together (kEmbeddingLengthBucketMaxRatio) to
#    bound padding-induced numerical drift. Result on a real 24.6-minute
#    recording: 330.1s (CPU) -> 148.4s (CUDA, unbatched) -> ~33s (CUDA,
#    batched).
#
# 2. Report real, continuous progress across the WHOLE diarize() call —
#    every phase (segmentation, label conversion, feature extraction,
#    batched inference) invokes a progress callback once per unit of real
#    completed work, blended into one combined 0..1000 counter in
#    Process().
#
# 3. Bound peak CPU memory in the embedding phase to a constant instead of
#    scaling with file length: process in bounded windows
#    (kEmbeddingStreamWindowSize) instead of building an OnlineStream (with
#    its extracted fbank features held in memory) for every segment in the
#    whole file up front. A multi-gigabyte allocation there was getting
#    silently caught by DiarizationEngine::diarize()'s own graceful-
#    degradation try/catch — "speakers silently stop being written, no
#    error shown" on a real 4h48m recording.
#
# 4. GPU memory robustness, in three parts, all motivated by the SAME real
#    4h48m recording still failing after (3):
#      a. kEmbeddingMaxBatchFrames: a soft cap on batch_count * max_frames
#         (the actual driver of a batch's GPU tensor size), to avoid
#         attempting allocations likely to fail. Explicitly NOT a
#         guarantee — an earlier, more generous value here still let
#         through a batch that triggered a real 1.1 GB single-allocation
#         CUDA failure.
#      b. ProcessEmbeddingBatch() catches that failure and bisects the
#         batch, retrying each half — recursing down to single items if
#         needed, with a per-item failure marked NaN (filtered out via the
#         existing "embedding model may output NaN" handling) rather than
#         propagating. This is the real safety net: no fixed cap can be
#         guaranteed correct, since available GPU memory depends on
#         whatever else is using the GPU at the time. Without this, ONE
#         oversized batch failed the ENTIRE diarize() call via
#         DiarizationEngine's top-level catch, discarding every other
#         segment's already-completed work too. The CUDA OOM itself was
#         already being caught gracefully, but left the GPU pressured
#         enough that a LATER, unrelated whisper.cpp Vulkan allocation
#         also failed — and THAT failure is uncaught anywhere in
#         whisper.cpp/ggml, terminating the whole process. Fixing the
#         diarization-side root cause prevents the downstream crash too.
#      c. kEmbeddingFrameQuantum: max_frames is rounded UP to the nearest
#         quantum instead of using each bucket's raw longest item. Why:
#         ONNX Runtime's CUDA memory arena is a CACHING allocator — it
#         holds distinctly-sized buffers for reuse rather than freeing
#         them, so a long recording's thousands of buckets (each with a
#         slightly different raw max_frames) were asking it to cache
#         thousands of uniquely-sized tensors. Confirmed directly: this
#         grew the arena to ~29 GB (nearly the entire 32 GB card) on the
#         same real recording, before the 1.1 GB failure in (a)/(b). Also
#         fixed a genuine bug where windowed processing (3) changed
#         execution order but the progress-numbering scheme still assumed
#         the old order, causing progress to visibly jump forward then
#         back at every window boundary ("jumps back and forth between 20
#         and 40 percent") — verified via a regression check forcing ~27
#         window transitions on the 24-minute file: 5282 progress events,
#         zero backward jumps.
#
# 5. arena_extend_strategy = kSameAsRequested (1) for the CUDA execution
#    provider (session.cc), instead of onnxruntime's own default (0 =
#    kNextPowerOfTwo). Motivated by a real log line: "Failed to allocate
#    memory for requested buffer of size 917504000" (~875 MB) inside a
#    Conv node's BFCArena allocation, still occurring after (4) — the
#    default strategy rounds each arena extension up to a power-of-two-
#    sized chunk, so a moderate request can trigger a cudaMalloc for far
#    more than what's actually needed. That's especially costly here
#    because this process ALSO runs whisper.cpp's own independent CUDA
#    allocator concurrently (diarization runs while a warm whisper.cpp
#    model sits GPU-resident) — two allocators fragmenting one device's
#    address space means an oversized request can fail even when
#    aggregate free VRAM is plentiful. kSameAsRequested allocates exactly
#    the requested size instead; this is onnxruntime's own documented
#    mitigation for this exact error.
#
# 6. Diagnostic-only: log batch_count/max_frames/feat_dim/bytes at the top
#    of ProcessEmbeddingBatch, gated behind SUOP_LOG_EMBEDDING_BATCHES so it
#    costs nothing by default. Added to pin down whether (4a)'s uncapped
#    batch_count==1 case (a single anomalously long segment) is what still
#    produces the ~901MB/~918MB allocation failures seen AFTER (5) landed —
#    (5) fixes how a request is rounded, not whether an oversized request is
#    made in the first place.
#
# Points 1-5 were originally diffed against pristine sherpa-onnx v1.13.4
# (submodule commit 142807252687d81b40d6315f23470a1512a00de3) and applied at
# configure time. They are now baked directly into the pinned submodule
# commit itself (7398790fbc6da22f67b9fc1990eb3397a1974ec1, on the
# suopuwu/sherpa-onnx fork — see that submodule's own "Use gpu better" /
# "Update session.cc" commits) rather than living as an applied-at-
# configure-time diff, so this file's diff body below no longer carries
# their hunks: re-applying a pristine-baselined hunk against a tree that
# already has it committed natively fails ("patch does not apply"), and
# `git apply --reverse --check` cannot report "already applied" for content
# it never touches. Only point 6 (below) is a live diff, applied at
# configure time on top of that pinned commit — re-verify with `git apply
# --check` after any submodule bump, same as before; if the submodule pin
# ever reverts to real upstream v1.13.4, re-derive the whole thing from
# points 1-5's numbered description instead of trying to reconstruct hunks
# from this history.
